 ## The taxable base
 
-**Employee social base.** Pay from the work, except income that is not taxable or is exempt from income tax, and except employer contributions to supplementary pension saving (Act 461/2003 §138(1)). A profit share paid to an employee who holds no capital is included. The base is capped at EUR 16,764 a month; employer accident insurance runs on the full pay (§138(10)). From 2026 the employee and employer pay social insurance on any pay accounted during sickness, maternity or care leave. The old exclusion periods are gone for employees (Sociálna poisťovňa, changes from 1 January 2026, item 26). Where someone has several jobs, social contributions run from the highest base down (§138(12)).
+**Employee social base.** Pay from the work, except income that is not taxable or is exempt from income tax, and except employer contributions to supplementary pension saving (Act 461/2003 §138(1)). The income-tax exemption does not remove qualifying registered-trainer income from the social base: that income is included before its separate trainer deduction. For an employee's qualifying legal relationship as a trainer entered in the register of natural persons in sport, §138c gives a EUR 300 monthly deduction, limited to that relationship's assessment base if lower. Employer accident and guarantee insurance use the employee base before that deduction (§138(6)); do not remove the deduction from those bases. A profit share paid to an employee who holds no capital is included. The base is capped at EUR 16,764 a month; employer accident insurance runs on the full pay (§138(10)). For remuneration for 2026 periods, the employee and employer pay social insurance on assessment-base income accounted during sickness, maternity or care leave: the old employee exclusion periods are abolished. If pay is accounted after 31 December 2025 for a period before 1 January 2026 containing the then-excluded days, adjust the base under the law effective through 31 December 2025, as the authority's transition instruction requires (Sociálna poisťovňa, changes from 1 January 2026, item 26). Where someone has several jobs, social contributions run from the highest base down (§138(12)).
 
 **Partial-month social insurance.** The monthly figures above assume a full contribution month. If an employee's sickness, pension and unemployment insurance lasts only part of the calendar month, adjust the §138(9) ceiling for the contribution days. For a compulsorily insured SZČO, adjust the applicable income-derived or special base and its applicable minimum/maximum under §138(2), (3), (8), (9) when insurance lasts only part of the month or §140 excludes contribution days. Divide the applicable monthly base by the actual calendar days in that month, round the daily base down to cents, then multiply by the days for which contributions are payable; round each separate fund contribution down to cents. This does not create an employee social minimum or restore the employee sickness/maternity exclusion abolished in 2026. The employer base follows §138(5)/(6), with the separate §138(10) ceiling and uncapped accident insurance. For concurrent activities, §138(12) gives employee income priority over compulsory SZČO income; among employee bases, take the highest first and, if equal, the insurance that started earlier, subject to the aggregate §138(9) ceiling. For example, the authority's May 2026 SZČO case uses EUR 1200 ÷31 → EUR 38.70 daily, then ×20 payable days = EUR 774 base and EUR 256.57 across the four funds, rather than prorating the final monthly payment ([Act 461/2003 §138(12)–(14)](https://static.slov-lex.sk/static/SK/ZZ/2003/461/20260901.html#paragraf-138); [SP partial-month instructions and worked case](https://www.socpoist.sk/socialne-poistenie/platenie-poistneho/poistne-szco-dpo-po-prerusenom-poisteni), read 2026-10-27).
 
-**Employee health base.** The same employment income, with no ceiling (Act 580/2004 §13(1); VšZP, employer page). The employer's 11% runs on the gross before any deductible amount (§13(4), §16(2)(c)). Start with the EUR 45.45 combined minimum only for a full month without excluded days. Under §16a(2), reduce that minimum proportionately for calendar days when the person is self-employed, state-insured under §11(7)/(8), disabled, declared missing, or not an employee. Count each excluded calendar day once. Compare the reduced minimum with the actual employee-plus-employer advance, after the applicable deductible amount; only a positive shortfall increases the employee share (§16a(3)/(4)). An employee with concurrent employers can elect not to apply that shortfall increase and must notify the employer within eight days of the employment/concurrency starting, and within eight days of a change. The employee also communicates excluded-day status where another body is not required to notify it; retain the prescribed written notice (§16a(5)). With EUR 200 pay, no deductible amount and no excluded days, ordinary advances are EUR 10 employee plus EUR 22 employer; the EUR 13.45 shortfall increases the employee advance to EUR 23.45. If the same person is state-insured for the entire month, the minimum reduces to zero: actual advances remain EUR 10 and EUR 22, with no minimum shortfall. State-insured status does not itself erase the contributions on employment ([Health Act §16a](https://static.slov-lex.sk/static/SK/ZZ/2004/580/20260701.html#paragraf-16a)).
+**Employee health base.** Employment income under the health law's own §10b and §13(1), with no ceiling (Act 580/2004; VšZP, employer page). The employer's 11% runs on the gross before any deductible amount (§13(4), §16(2)(c)). Start with the EUR 45.45 combined minimum only for a full month without excluded days. Under §16a(2), reduce that minimum proportionately for calendar days when the person is self-employed, state-insured under §11(7)/(8), disabled, declared missing, or not an employee. Count each excluded calendar day once. Compare the reduced minimum with the actual employee-plus-employer advance, after the applicable deductible amount; only a positive shortfall increases the employee share (§16a(3)/(4)). An employee with concurrent employers can elect not to apply that shortfall increase and must notify the employer within eight days of the employment/concurrency starting, and within eight days of a change. The employee also communicates excluded-day status where another body is not required to notify it; retain the prescribed written notice (§16a(5)). With EUR 200 pay, no deductible amount and no excluded days, ordinary advances are EUR 10 employee plus EUR 22 employer; the EUR 13.45 shortfall increases the employee advance to EUR 23.45. If the same person is state-insured for the entire month, the minimum reduces to zero: actual advances remain EUR 10 and EUR 22, with no minimum shortfall. State-insured status does not itself erase the contributions on employment ([Health Act §16a](https://static.slov-lex.sk/static/SK/ZZ/2004/580/20260701.html#paragraf-16a)).
 
 **Wage tax advance.** Taxable wage = gross taxable pay less the employee's social and health contributions, less EUR 497.23 if the employee has signed the declaration (vyhlásenie) with this employer (Act 595/2003 §35(1), (4), §36(6)). Only one employer may hold that declaration. The spouse allowance and pension savings deductions wait for the annual reconciliation (§35(1)(b)). Apply the monthly bands to the taxable wage, then reduce the advance by the child tax bonus (§35(2), (5)).
 
